    "source": "Münster, St.-Lamberti-Kirche -- 2017 -- 9784",
    "description": "Signs of Way of Saint James at the St. Lamberti Church, Münster, North Rhine-Westphalia, GermanySt. Lamberti is a Roman Catholic parish church in Münster’s old town. It was built between 1375 and 1525 as a market and civic church and is located on Lambertikirchplatz and at the corner of Salzstraße and Prinzipalmarkt. St. Lamberti is the most important sacred building of the Westphalian late Gothic period. It is named after St. Lambert of Liège.A special feature are three iron baskets attached to the tower, in which the bodies of the three leaders of the Anabaptist kingdom of Münster, Jan van Leiden, Bernd Krechting, and Bernd Knipperdolling, were displayed in 1536 after they had been publicly tortured and executed in the square in front of the church.From 9 p. m. to midnight, a tower watchman or watchwoman blows a horn every half hour. The office has existed since 1379.",
    "dateCreated": "Taken on 21 April 2017, 12:29",
